# 10. Diagnosing EC2-A → EC2-B

We tested connectivity from EC2-A to EC2-B.

Initially:

```bash
nc -vz -w 5 172.31.16.13 22
```

returned a timeout.

This was expected because EC2-B's Security Group did not allow SSH from EC2-A.

At this point, we temporarily added:

```text
TCP 22
Source = saa-lab-1-a
```

to EC2-B's Security Group.

Then:

```bash
nc -vz -w 5 172.31.16.13 22
```

returned:

```text
Connection to 172.31.16.13 22 port [tcp/ssh] succeeded!
```

This was an important diagnostic result.

It proved:

* EC2-A could reach EC2-B at the network level.
* EC2-B's SSH daemon was reachable.
* The VPC routing path was working.
* The previous timeout was caused by the Security Group rule.

---

# 11. SSH Agent Forwarding

We connected to EC2-A using SSH agent forwarding:

```bash
ssh -A -i ~/.ssh/aws/saa-lab-1.pem admin@<public-ip>
```

Then, from EC2-A:

```bash
ssh admin@172.31.16.13
```

Initially this produced:

```text
Permission denied (publickey)
```

After configuring/using SSH agent forwarding correctly, the connection succeeded:

```text
admin@ip-172-31-16-13:~$
```

Therefore we were able to reach EC2-B through EC2-A.

